---
import Sub from '../layouts/Sub.astro';
import { servicePageSchema } from '../data/site.js';

const description =
  'Wet-look and decorative concrete sealing in St. George, Utah. Colour deepened from within the surface, or a high-shine finish on top — shown on your own concrete before you decide.';

const schema = servicePageSchema({
  path: '/wet-look-concrete-sealing',
  name: 'Wet-look and decorative concrete sealing',
  description,
  crumb: 'Wet-look & decorative',
});
---

<Sub
  title="Wet-Look & Decorative Sealing | St. George UT | Armour Crete"
  description={description}
  crumb="Wet-look & decorative"
  schema={schema}
>
  <Fragment slot="h1">The colour it has<br />when it rains.</Fragment>
  <Fragment slot="lede">
    A wet-look finish deepens the colour of concrete, pavers or stone and holds
    it there — the way the surface looks in the first minute of a storm, kept.
    There are two honest ways to get there, and they age very differently, so we
    show you both on your own concrete before you choose.
  </Fragment>
  <Fragment slot="aside">
    <dl class="facts">
      <div><dt>2</dt><dd>Ways to a wet look — from within, or as a shine on top</dd></div>
      <div><dt>UV</dt><dd>Protection in both — the desert sun is the main event</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Demo</dt><dd>Shown on your own surface before you commit</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Free</dt><dd>On-site assessment, no obligation</dd></div>
    </dl>
  </Fragment>

  <section class="sec">
    <div class="wrap split">
      <div class="rv">
        <p class="eyebrow">The honest distinction</p>
        <h2 class="display sec-h">From within,<br />or on top.</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="prose rv">
        <p>
          <strong>SilaCast BES</strong> deepens the material's own colour from
          below. It penetrates porous surfaces — concrete pavers, roof and barrel
          tile, natural stone — rather than laying a film on top, it carries two
          forms of UV protection, and it never needs stripping before it can be
          re-coated. A topical film can yellow under UV, it can peel, and taking
          one off means harsh chemicals; a penetrating enhancer has none of those
          problems, because there is no film to fail.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Shine-A-Cast</strong> is the other road: a hybrid that penetrates
          to enhance colour and also leaves a protective layer on the surface.
          That layer is what produces the high shine, what locks joint sand in
          place on paver work, and what brings a sun-faded surface back — pigment
          can even be added to tune the colour. It is restricted to approved
          applicators, which we are.
        </p>
        <p>
          Which one belongs on your property depends on the surface, the sun it
          takes, and how you use it — a high-shine layer next to a pool is a
          conversation about <a class="tlink" href="/pool-deck-sealing">slip
          resistance</a> before it is a conversation about looks.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="sec sec-dark">
    <div class="wrap split">
      <div class="rv">
        <p class="eyebrow eyebrow-lt">Where it earns its keep</p>
        <h2 class="display sec-h">Made for<br />feature surfaces.</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="rv">
        <ul class="dotlist">
          <li>Paver driveways and entries — colour up, joint sand locked</li>
          <li>Decorative patios and courtyards</li>
          <li>Exposed aggregate the builder got right — lifted, not hidden</li>
          <li>Sun-faded surfaces that need their colour back</li>
          <li>Roof tile and retaining walls, enhanced from within</li>
        </ul>
        <p class="note" style="border-color: rgba(244,240,233,0.3); color: rgba(244,240,233,0.6);">
          If you want protection with no change in appearance at all, that is a
          different product doing a quieter job — see
          <a class="tlink" href="/driveway-sealing">driveway sealing</a>.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="sec">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="sec-head rv">
        <p class="eyebrow">Decorative sealing questions</p>
        <h2 class="display sec-h">Decided at<br />the demo.</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="faq rv">
        <details class="qa">
          <summary>How dark will it go?</summary>
          <div class="qa-b"><p>
            That depends on the surface — how porous it is and what colour is
            already in it. Which is exactly why we demo it on your own concrete
            first: you see the actual result, not a brochure's version of it.
          </p></div>
        </details>
        <details class="qa">
          <summary>Will it yellow in the sun?</summary>
          <div class="qa-b"><p>
            Yellowing is a film problem, and it is why we lean on penetrating
            products where they fit. SilaCast enhances from within the substrate
            and carries two types of UV protection; Shine-A-Cast's surface layer is
            UV protected as well. What we will not do is put down a bargain acrylic
            film and let the desert have it.
          </p></div>
        </details>
        <details class="qa">
          <summary>How long before I can use the surface?</summary>
          <div class="qa-b"><p>
            Plan for twenty-four hours, the same as any sealing work we do.
          </p></div>
        </details>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Sub>
